#metadata(title = "Longform Columns", author = "Mote")
#document(
  default_font = "body",
  default_size = pt(11),
  page = { size = "letter", orientation = "portrait" },
  margin = { top = pt(54), right = pt(60), bottom = pt(54), left = pt(60), header = pt(24), footer = pt(24) }
)
#font(name = "body", latin = "Aptos")
#style(name = "Normal", type = "paragraph", run = { font = "body", size = pt(11) }, paragraph = { spacing = { after = pt(7), line = pt(14) } })
#style(name = "Heading1", type = "paragraph", based_on = "Normal", run = { font = "body", size = pt(22), bold = true }, paragraph = { spacing = { before = pt(10), after = pt(8), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } }, keep_with_next = true })
#style(name = "Heading2", type = "paragraph", based_on = "Normal", run = { font = "body", size = pt(16), bold = true }, paragraph = { spacing = { before = pt(10), after = pt(6), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } }, keep_with_next = true })

#header(distance = pt(18), height = pt(18))[Mote Column Fixture #tab() Page #page_number()]
#footer(distance = pt(18))[Generated golden document]

# Intro

The opening material belongs to the implicit one-column section. It exists to make sure a document can begin naturally with markdown and still transition into a more explicit office layout.

#section(
  properties = { start = "next_page", vertical_alignment = "top" },
  columns = { count = 2, gap = pt(30), separator = true },
  page_numbering = { restart = true, start = 1 }
)[
  ## Left Column

  Mote sections can carry page-level layout without forcing the rest of the document to become verbose. This section intentionally contains enough text to exercise column flow in Word print layout.

  The parser should accept ordinary paragraphs, nested inline marks, and list syntax inside the section body. The emitter should leave these as editable Word paragraphs rather than drawing a fixed layout.

  - Plain markdown remains the starting point.
  - Directives add layout only where the author needs them.
    - Nested list items keep their hierarchy.
    - Inline `code`, **strong text**, and _emphasis_ stay inside the item.
  - A column break can move the following content to the next column.

  #break(type = "column")

  ## Right Column

  The second column starts after a real Word column break. This is useful for policies, brochures, contract terms, and other documents where columns are a section-level choice rather than a document-wide rule.

  Keep this text editable. Keep the section properties local. Keep the source readable enough that an LLM can generate it without needing to memorize hidden schema details.

  > A blockquote inside a two-column section should still behave like normal content.
]

#section(properties = { start = "next_page" }, columns = { count = 1 })[
  # Final Single Column Section

  The following section returns to one column without changing document defaults. This is the expected progressive path: markdown first, then layout directives only where they are useful.
]
